QB.push(
{id:1471,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"What values may the `type` field inside a `cache_control` object currently take?",
o:["A. Only `ephemeral`","B. `ephemeral` or `persistent`","C. `ephemeral`, `session`, or `permanent`","D. `static` for tools and `ephemeral` for messages"],a:0,
e:"`cache_control` currently accepts exactly one type: **`ephemeral`**. Durability is not expressed by a different type — it is expressed by the optional `ttl` field on the same object, which takes the default 5-minute behaviour or `\"1h\"`. So `{\"type\":\"ephemeral\"}` and `{\"type\":\"ephemeral\",\"ttl\":\"1h\"}` are the two shapes you will write. Naming it *ephemeral* is a deliberate signal: a cache entry is a short-lived cost and latency optimisation, never storage you may depend on. Your code must stay correct when every single request is a miss.",
w:"B — there is no `persistent` type; longer retention is requested with `ttl`, not a new type. C — neither `session` nor `permanent` exists; caches are not bound to a session object and nothing is permanent. D — the type does not vary by region; tools, system, and message blocks all use `ephemeral`."},

{id:1472,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"What does adding a single `cache_control` field at the **top level** of a Messages request do?",
o:["A. It caches only the `system` parameter and leaves `messages` uncached","B. It automatically applies the cache breakpoint to the last cacheable block and moves that breakpoint forward as the conversation grows","C. It caches the model's response as well as the prompt prefix","D. It removes the four-breakpoint limit for that request"],a:1,
e:"The top-level `cache_control` is the **automatic caching** convenience. Instead of hand-placing `cache_control` on a specific content block, you declare it once on the request and the system marks the **last cacheable block**, advancing that position as history accumulates. In a multi-turn chat this is exactly the behaviour you want: each request reads everything through the previous turn and writes the newest turn, so turn *n*'s write becomes turn *n+1*'s read. It consumes one of the four breakpoint slots and composes with explicit block-level breakpoints.",
w:"A — it is not scoped to `system`; the marked block is the last cacheable block anywhere in the tools, system, messages sequence. C — caching only ever covers the input prefix; generated output is never cached and never discounted. D — the four-breakpoint ceiling still applies, and automatic caching itself occupies one of those slots."},

{id:1473,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An agent request already carries four explicit `cache_control` breakpoints: one on the last tool definition, two inside `system`, and one on the last settled history block. A developer adds a top-level `cache_control` so the newest turn is cached automatically too. Every request now returns `400`.\n\nWhat is the cause?",
o:["A. Top-level `cache_control` and block-level `cache_control` are mutually exclusive in any request","B. Only three block-level breakpoints are permitted once automatic caching is requested","C. Automatic caching needs one of the four breakpoint slots and all four are already taken","D. The top-level field is only valid when `tools` is absent from the request"],a:2,
e:"A request may carry at most **four** cache breakpoints in total. Automatic caching is not exempt from that budget — it **consumes one slot** to place its own breakpoint on the last cacheable block. With four explicit block-level breakpoints already declared there is no slot left, and the API returns **`400`**. The fix is to drop the least valuable explicit breakpoint (often a mid-`system` split) or abandon the top-level field and move that fourth explicit breakpoint onto the newest turn yourself.",
w:"A — the two forms compose; automatic caching is documented as compatible with explicit breakpoints. B — the ceiling is four in total, not three plus one; three explicit plus automatic is legal precisely because it totals four. D — automatic caching has no dependency on the presence or absence of `tools`."},

{id:1474,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A `system` array holds two text blocks: a 900-token tone-of-voice guide first, then a 14,000-token policy manual. The developer marks the **first** block with `cache_control: {\"type\":\"ephemeral\"}` and reports that spend barely moved.\n\nWhy?",
o:["A. Only the final block of `system` is ever eligible for `cache_control`","B. The policy manual is cached but billed at the write rate on every request","C. Marking any `system` block caches the whole `system` array, so something else must be wrong","D. The cached prefix ends at the marked block, so only the 900-token guide is cached and the manual is re-billed as ordinary input every request"],a:3,
e:"A breakpoint caches the **prefix**: everything from the start of the request up to *and including* the marked block. Nothing after it is covered. Marking the 900-token block caches 900 tokens and leaves 14,000 tokens charged as full-price input on every call — a rounding error against the bill. The rule to internalise: place `cache_control` on the **last** block whose content is identical across the requests you want to share an entry, not on the first stable thing you happen to see. Here that means marking the policy manual.",
w:"A — any cacheable block may carry `cache_control`; there is no restriction to the final one. B — content outside the prefix is billed as ordinary input, not at the write rate; only prefix content is ever written. C — marking a block does not implicitly extend coverage forwards; the prefix stops at the marked block."},

{id:1475,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A developer serialises the request body with the `messages` key first, then `system`, then `tools`, believing JSON key order determines what falls inside the cached prefix. Cache behaviour is identical to a body written in the conventional order.\n\nWhy?",
o:["A. The SDK silently reorders the keys to match whatever order the previous request used","B. The cacheable prefix is assembled in a fixed canonical order — `tools`, then `system`, then `messages` — regardless of key order in the JSON object","C. Key order does change the prefix, but the difference is too small to observe","D. Key order matters only when more than one breakpoint is present"],a:1,
e:"The prefix is built from the request's logical regions in a fixed order: **`tools` → `system` → `messages`**. JSON objects are unordered by specification, so the serialisation order of top-level keys carries no meaning. What *does* matter is order **within** an ordered array — the sequence of tool definitions, of `system` blocks, and of `messages` and their content blocks. That is where instability actually bites. The transferable point: stabilise the arrays and stop worrying about the object.",
w:"A — no SDK rewrites your body to match a previous request; there is nothing to reorder because the regions already have a canonical order. C — the effect is exactly zero, not merely small; the prefix is unchanged. D — the number of breakpoints has no bearing on how top-level keys are interpreted."},

{id:1476,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"With caching active, what does the `input_tokens` field in the response `usage` object represent?",
o:["A. Only the tokens that appear after the last cache breakpoint in the request","B. Every input token sent, including cached ones","C. The tokens that were eligible for caching but fell below the model minimum","D. The tokens written to the cache during this request"],a:0,
e:"`input_tokens` counts **only the tokens after your last breakpoint** — the uncacheable tail. The three input figures partition the request: `cache_read_input_tokens` (in the prefix, served from an existing entry), `cache_creation_input_tokens` (in the prefix, written this request), and `input_tokens` (past the breakpoint). So `total_input_tokens = cache_read_input_tokens + cache_creation_input_tokens + input_tokens`. Teams that graph `input_tokens` alone after enabling caching watch it collapse and wrongly conclude their traffic vanished; it merely moved into the other two counters.",
w:"B — cached tokens are reported separately and are excluded from `input_tokens`. C — a sub-minimum prefix is billed as ordinary input and does appear here, but that is not what the field means. D — writes are reported in `cache_creation_input_tokens`, a different field."},

{id:1477,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"After enabling caching, a cost dashboard that sums `usage.input_tokens` per call shows an 85% drop in input volume. The invoice falls by only about 60%.\n\nWhat is wrong with the dashboard?",
o:["A. Cache reads are billed at the full input rate, so the dashboard overstates the saving","B. The invoice lags the dashboard by one billing cycle","C. The dashboard ignores `cache_read_input_tokens` and `cache_creation_input_tokens`, which are billed at roughly 0.1× and 1.25× of the base input rate respectively","D. `input_tokens` double-counts cached tokens, so the true drop is larger than 85%"],a:2,
e:"`input_tokens` covers only the tail past the breakpoint. Cached tokens are reported in **`cache_read_input_tokens`** and **`cache_creation_input_tokens`**, and both are billed — reads at roughly **0.1×** the base input rate, writes at **1.25×** (2× for a 1-hour TTL). A dashboard summing `input_tokens` alone reports the tokens it stopped paying full price for as though they had become free. Correct instrumentation computes a weighted figure across all three counters using the multipliers, then reconciles that against the invoice.",
w:"A — reads are heavily discounted, not full price; if they were full price caching would never pay. B — the discrepancy is structural, not a timing artefact, and it reproduces every cycle. D — there is no double counting; the three counters are disjoint partitions of the input."},

{id:1478,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team adds `cache_control` to a 400-token system prompt on a high-volume endpoint. No error is returned, spend is unchanged, and `usage` shows `cache_creation_input_tokens: 0` and `cache_read_input_tokens: 0` on every call.\n\nWhat happened?",
o:["A. The breakpoint was rejected and a `400` is being swallowed by their HTTP client","B. The cache is working, but the counters only populate from the second request onward","C. The endpoint's traffic is too concurrent for entries to be reused","D. The prefix is below the model's minimum cacheable length, so the request was processed without caching and no error was raised"],a:3,
e:"Every model has a **minimum cacheable prompt length**, and it **varies by model**. A prefix shorter than that minimum is simply processed **without caching** — no error, no warning, no write charge. Both counters read `0`, which is the documented signature of \"caching did not occur\". Because the value differs per model, treat it as something to check in the docs for the exact model you deploy rather than as a constant. Diagnostic habit: after enabling caching, assert that at least one of the two counters is non-zero.",
w:"A — sub-minimum prefixes are handled silently; there is no error to swallow. B — a first request populates `cache_creation_input_tokens`, so a genuinely caching endpoint never shows zero on both counters indefinitely. C — concurrency causes extra *writes*, not zeroes on both counters."},

{id:1479,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Which statement about the minimum cacheable prompt length is correct?",
o:["A. It varies by model and is not configurable per request","B. It is a fixed 1,024 tokens across every Claude model","C. It can be lowered with a request parameter for short prompts","D. Prefixes below it return `400 invalid_request_error`"],a:0,
e:"The threshold is a **per-model property**: different models publish different minimums, and no request parameter lowers it. Below the minimum the request is served **without caching and without an error**, so the failure mode is silent and visible only as `cache_creation_input_tokens` and `cache_read_input_tokens` both sitting at `0`. Practical consequence: a prefix that caches on one model may stop caching after a migration. Look up the published minimum for the specific model, and monitor the counters rather than assuming.",
w:"B — there is no single universal figure; published minimums differ across models and tiers. C — no parameter adjusts the threshold. D — nothing is rejected; sub-minimum prefixes are processed normally with caching skipped."},

{id:1480,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A summarisation service caches a 1,800-token instruction-plus-few-shot prefix and has run at a ~92% hit rate for months. To cut cost the team migrates to a cheaper, smaller model. Latency and quality are acceptable, but the cache counters now read `0`/`0` and input spend per call rises.\n\nWhat is the most likely explanation?",
o:["A. The cheaper model does not support prompt caching at all","B. The new model's minimum cacheable prompt length is higher than 1,800 tokens, so the prefix is silently no longer cached","C. Entries written under the old model were being reused and have now expired permanently","D. Smaller models bill cache reads at the full input rate"],a:1,
e:"Minimum cacheable length is **model-specific**, and some cheaper or smaller models publish a *higher* minimum than flagship ones. A prefix that comfortably cleared the old bar can fall under the new one, at which point the request is served **without caching and without any error** — precisely the `0`/`0` signature. The migration lesson: a model swap changes caching economics, not just per-token price. Re-verify the minimum, and consider whether extending the stable prefix with genuinely useful content clears the new threshold.",
w:"A — caching is broadly available across models; what differs is minimum length and the read multiplier, not availability. C — expiry would show reads falling to zero while writes continued, and entries do not carry across a model change anyway. D — reads remain steeply discounted; no tier bills them at the full input rate."},

{id:1481,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team runs every call with `\"stream\": true` and reports they cannot verify caching because the final `message_stop` event carries no cache figures.\n\nWhere do the cache counters appear in a streamed response?",
o:["A. Caching is unavailable when streaming is enabled","B. In the `message_delta` event alongside the final `stop_reason`","C. In the `usage` object on the `message_start` event","D. Only via a follow-up call to `/v1/messages/count_tokens`"],a:2,
e:"Streaming and caching compose freely. Input accounting — including **`cache_creation_input_tokens`** and **`cache_read_input_tokens`** — is known before any token is generated, so it arrives in the **`usage` object on `message_start`**. What arrives later, in `message_delta`, is the final `stop_reason` and the cumulative *output* usage. A consumer that inspects only the tail of the stream therefore sees output accounting and no cache accounting. Instrument both: `message_start` for input and cache metrics, `message_delta` for output and stop reason.",
w:"A — caching works normally with streaming; only the reporting location differs. B — `message_delta` carries the final `stop_reason` and cumulative output usage, not the cache write and read counters. D — `count_tokens` returns only `input_tokens` and does not exercise or report caching."},

{id:1482,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An agent loop keeps one breakpoint on the final content block of `messages`. Early turns hit the cache reliably. From about turn four onward each turn issues 12–15 parallel tool calls plus their results, and hits stop entirely — even though nothing earlier in the conversation was edited.\n\nWhat is happening, and what fixes it?",
o:["A. Parallel tool calls cannot be cached; set `disable_parallel_tool_use: true`","B. `tool_result` blocks reset the cache on arrival; move the breakpoint into `system`","C. The prefix exceeded the model's maximum cacheable length; shorten the history","D. Each turn now adds enough blocks to push the breakpoint past the 20-position lookback window, so add a second breakpoint further back where a write can accumulate in advance"],a:3,
e:"A cache read is found by **lookback**: the system hashes the prefix at your breakpoint and, on a miss, walks backward one position at a time for at most **20 positions**. A turn that adds more blocks than remain in that window leaves the previous request's entry just outside it, so nothing is found even though the earlier content is byte-identical and still live. A **second breakpoint further back** opens its own lookback window there and, crucially, accumulates a write at that position before you need it. Mitigating detail: a run of consecutive `tool_use` blocks counts as one position, as does a run of consecutive `tool_result` blocks.",
w:"A — parallel tool calls are cacheable, and consecutive runs of them collapse to a single lookback position; disabling parallelism trades throughput for nothing. B — `tool_result` blocks reset nothing, and retreating into `system` abandons history caching entirely. C — no maximum cacheable prefix length is in play; the constraint is the lookback window, not size."},

{id:1483,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"R",f:"H",
q:"When the cache lookback walks backward from a breakpoint, how are consecutive `tool_use` blocks and consecutive `tool_result` blocks counted on the Claude API?",
o:["A. A run of consecutive `tool_use` blocks counts as one position, and so does a run of consecutive `tool_result` blocks","B. Each individual block counts as one position","C. Tool blocks are skipped entirely and never counted","D. Tool blocks count double because they carry structured JSON"],a:0,
e:"The lookback window is **20 positions**, counting the breakpoint itself as the first. To stop parallel tool use from exhausting it, a **run of consecutive `tool_use` blocks counts as a single position**, and likewise for a run of consecutive `tool_result` blocks. That is why a turn issuing a dozen simultaneous tool calls does not, on its own, push the previous request's entry out of range. It does not make the window infinite: text blocks, interleaved turns, and successive tool rounds still consume positions, so long agent turns can still outrun it and need an extra breakpoint further back.",
w:"B — per-block counting is exactly what the run-collapsing rule avoids; a parallel fan-out would otherwise blow the window in a single turn. C — tool blocks are cacheable content and do occupy a position; they are not skipped. D — no block type is weighted double."},

{id:1484,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A latency-sensitive assistant wants its 40,000-token system prompt already in cache before the first user message arrives. The team sends a request with `max_tokens: 0`, the system prompt marked with `cache_control`, and a throwaway user message.\n\nWhat should they expect in the response?",
o:["A. A `400`, because `max_tokens` must be at least 1 on every request","B. An empty `content` array, `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"`, and a fully populated `usage` block showing the cache write","C. A normal completion, since `max_tokens: 0` is treated as unbounded","D. A `200` with no `usage` object, because nothing was processed"],a:1,
e:"**Cache pre-warming** is a supported pattern: `max_tokens: 0` makes the API read the prompt and write the cache at every `cache_control` breakpoint, then return immediately without generating. The response has an **empty `content` array**, **`stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"`**, and a **fully populated `usage` block** — zero output tokens billed, with `cache_creation_input_tokens` reflecting the write. This removes the cold-miss penalty from the first real interaction, the one that actually matters for time-to-first-token. It is not a substitute for a TTL long enough to cover your traffic cadence.",
w:"A — `max_tokens: 0` is specifically permitted for pre-warming on the Messages API; the at-least-1 rule applies inside Message Batches. C — zero is not a synonym for unbounded; generation is skipped entirely. D — `usage` is fully populated, which is the whole point of the call."},

{id:1485,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team pre-warms with `max_tokens: 0`. They attach `cache_control` to the throwaway user message `\"warmup\"` at the end of the request, reasoning the breakpoint should sit as late as possible. The warm-up reports a large `cache_creation_input_tokens`, but the first real user request is still a full cache miss.\n\nWhy?",
o:["A. Pre-warm entries are written to a separate cache namespace from live traffic","B. `cache_control` on a `user` message is ignored, so nothing was actually written","C. The written prefix ends with `\"warmup\"`, which no real request contains, so no real request can share that prefix","D. `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"` marks the entry invalid for reuse"],a:2,
e:"An entry is keyed by the **exact prefix up to and including the breakpoint**. Marking the placeholder turn makes `\"warmup\"` part of that prefix — and no genuine request ever contains it, so no genuine request can match. Place the breakpoint on the **last block real traffic also sends**, normally the final `system` block or the last tool definition; the placeholder then sits *after* the breakpoint where it cannot contaminate anything. The rule applies to ordinary caching too: the breakpoint must fall on shared content, not merely on late content.",
w:"A — there is no separate namespace; a pre-warm write is an ordinary write. B — `cache_control` is valid on user-turn content blocks, and the large `cache_creation_input_tokens` proves a write occurred. D — the stop reason is metadata about that response and has no bearing on entry validity."},

{id:1486,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"In which situation is `max_tokens: 0` **rejected**?",
o:["A. When the request carries `tools`","B. When more than one `cache_control` breakpoint is present","C. When the model is a smaller or cheaper tier","D. Inside a Message Batches request, and when streaming, extended thinking, structured outputs, or a forced `tool_choice` is in use"],a:3,
e:"`max_tokens: 0` exists for one narrow purpose — writing the cache without generating — so it is rejected wherever generation is structurally required or the pattern is meaningless. That includes **`stream: true`**, extended thinking, structured outputs, a `tool_choice` that forces a tool call, and **inside a Message Batches request**, where every request needs `max_tokens` of at least 1. The batch exclusion has a stated rationale: pre-warming targets time-to-first-token, which is irrelevant to asynchronous processing, and an entry written mid-batch would likely expire before anything reused it.",
w:"A — tool definitions are a prime pre-warm target; their presence is not a problem. B — multiple breakpoints are fine, and a pre-warm request writes at each of them. C — model tier does not gate the pattern."},

{id:1487,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A nightly job launches 50 workers simultaneously, all sending an identical 30,000-token cached prefix. The team expected one write and 49 reads. `usage` shows a cache write on nearly every worker.\n\nWhat explains it, and what is the cheapest correction?",
o:["A. A cache entry only becomes available once the first response has begun, so the concurrent fan-out all missed; send one request first, wait for its response to begin, then release the rest","B. Fifty concurrent requests exceed the per-entry read concurrency limit; add a semaphore capping reads at 10","C. The prefix was over the write size limit so each worker cached a different slice; split it across two breakpoints","D. Concurrent requests are load-balanced to different model replicas with independent caches; pin a replica via a header"],a:0,
e:"A cache entry **only becomes available after the first response begins**. Fifty requests issued in the same instant all find nothing, so all fifty write — you pay **1.25×** fifty times instead of once, which is worse than not caching at all for that wave. The correction is a **cold-start gate**: issue one request (or a `max_tokens: 0` pre-warm), wait for its response to begin, then release the remaining workers, which now read at roughly **0.1×**. The general lesson is that caching rewards *sequenced* repetition, and a thundering herd defeats it.",
w:"B — there is no per-entry read concurrency cap, and throttling reads would not have prevented the writes. C — the writes are simultaneous misses, not fragments of an oversized prefix; splitting breakpoints changes nothing here. D — there is no replica-pinning header, and entry availability timing already explains the behaviour."},

{id:1488,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A prefix is 20,000 tokens. In a five-minute window a service makes exactly two calls sharing it: the first writes the cache, the second reads it. Ignoring output cost, how does this compare with not caching, given a write at 1.25× and a read at roughly 0.1× the base input rate?",
o:["A. Worse; a single read cannot repay a 1.25× write","B. Better — about 1.35× the base cost of one prefix against 2× uncached, roughly a 32% saving","C. Exactly break-even","D. Not comparable, because writes are billed per entry rather than per token"],a:1,
e:"Work in units of \"one uncached prefix\". Uncached: 1 + 1 = **2.0**. Cached: a write at **1.25** plus a read at **0.1** = **1.35**. So even a *single* reuse inside the TTL is comfortably profitable, saving about a third. Break-even sits below one read, which means caching only loses money when a write is **never** read — in which case you pay 1.25 instead of 1.0, a 25% penalty on that prefix. That asymmetry is why enabling caching on any genuinely repeated prefix is close to a default decision, and why the real risk is prefix *instability* rather than insufficient volume.",
w:"A — 1.25 + 0.1 = 1.35 is well under the uncached 2.0, so one read repays the write with room to spare. C — break-even would require the cached total to equal 2.0, which no two-call scenario produces. D — cache writes and reads are billed per token with a multiplier, not as a flat per-entry fee."},

{id:1489,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A workload sends the same 50,000-token prefix on an irregular cadence, sometimes 25 minutes apart. The team switches to `ttl: \"1h\"`, which bills writes at 2× base input. Reads stay at roughly 0.1×.\n\nHow many reads per write does the 1-hour TTL need before it beats sending the prefix uncached?",
o:["A. Exactly one, the same as the 5-minute TTL","B. About eleven, since reads are a tenth of base","C. None; a 2× write is already cheaper than a 1× uncached send","D. At least two, because one write plus one read costs 2.1 against 2.0 uncached"],a:3,
e:"In units of one uncached prefix, an entry written at **2×** and read once costs **2 + 0.1 = 2.1** against **2.0** uncached — a marginal *loss*. Two reads flip it: **2 + 0.2 = 2.2** against **3.0**, a 27% saving. So the 1-hour TTL needs roughly **two or more reads per write**, where the 5-minute TTL needs only one. That is the whole decision: choose 1-hour when your cadence is too slow for a five-minute window but each entry will still be reused at least twice. If entries are typically read once, the cheaper 1.25× write is the better bet.",
w:"A — one read leaves the 1-hour entry slightly behind uncached, because its write costs 2× rather than 1.25×. B — the crossover is set by comparing `2 + 0.1N` against `N + 1`, which flips at two reads, not eleven. C — a 2× write is by definition dearer than a 1× uncached send; the saving comes from the discounted reads that follow it."},

{id:1490,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A service enables caching on a 12,000-token prefix, but each user session makes exactly one call and sessions are minutes apart. Input spend rises by about a quarter.\n\nWhat is the economic explanation?",
o:["A. Cache reads are being billed at the write rate because entries expire before use","B. The prefix is below the minimum, so writes are billed but never stored","C. Every request writes an entry that is never read, so each pays 1.25× base input instead of 1×","D. The 5-minute TTL is charged as a standing fee for each live entry"],a:2,
e:"Caching is a bet that a write will be read. Here it never is: each call pays the **write multiplier of 1.25×** and no call ever collects the **0.1×** read, so the bill rises by exactly the write premium — about **25%**. This is the one shape where caching reliably *loses* money, and it is a traffic-pattern problem, not a configuration problem. Either raise reuse (batch related work together, share a prefix across users rather than per session) or turn caching off for that endpoint. Signature to watch: `cache_creation_input_tokens` non-zero on essentially every call with `cache_read_input_tokens` at zero.",
w:"A — reads are never billed at the write rate; the issue is that no read occurs at all. B — a sub-minimum prefix is processed without caching and costs nothing extra, so spend would be flat rather than up 25%. D — there is no standing storage fee; you are billed for token writes and reads only."},

{id:1491,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"For a prefix written once and then read very many times within its TTL, what is the approximate ceiling on the input-cost saving for that cached portion?",
o:["A. About 90%, because reads are billed at roughly 0.1× the base input rate","B. 100%, because reads of a live entry are free","C. About 50%, matching the batch discount","D. About 75%, because writes cost 1.25×"],a:0,
e:"The asymptote is set by the **read multiplier**. With reads at roughly **0.1×** base input, a heavily reused prefix trends toward paying about a tenth of its uncached cost — roughly a **90% saving** on the cached portion, with the one-off **1.25×** write amortised to nothing. Two caveats worth carrying: the read multiplier is lower still on some of the newest models, so treat 0.1× as the common case rather than a constant; and the saving applies only to the cached prefix — tokens past the breakpoint and all output tokens are unaffected.",
w:"B — reads are discounted, not free; a live entry still bills read tokens. C — 50% is the Message Batches discount, an unrelated mechanism with its own ceiling. D — the write multiplier bounds the worst case, not the best; the ceiling is governed by the read multiplier."},

{id:1492,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An endpoint sends a 25,000-token stable prefix plus a ~500-token variable tail, 4,000 times a day, with calls close enough together that one entry serves roughly 200 calls before being rewritten. Using a write at 1.25× and reads at roughly 0.1×, what is the approximate change in **input** cost?",
o:["A. Roughly 50% lower, limited by the write premium","B. Roughly 88% lower on the prefix, with the ~500-token tail unchanged","C. Roughly 25% higher, because writes outnumber reads","D. Unchanged, because the variable tail forces a rewrite on every call"],a:1,
e:"Model one entry's life: 1 write + 199 reads over 25,000 tokens. Cached cost in prefix-units is `1.25 + 199 × 0.1 = 21.15` against `200` uncached — about **89% lower**, so ~88–90% on the prefix. The 500-token tail sits **after** the breakpoint and is billed as ordinary input regardless, which is why the blended saving lands slightly under the prefix figure. The transferable method: express cost per entry as `write_multiplier + reads × read_multiplier`, then divide by the read count. Only two inputs matter — the read multiplier and reads-per-write.",
w:"A — 50% understates it badly; with ~200 reads per write the 1.25× write amortises to well under 1% of the total. C — writes are vastly outnumbered here; the 25%-higher outcome is what happens when writes are never read. D — a variable tail placed after the breakpoint does not invalidate the prefix, which is precisely why it goes there."},

{id:1493,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Which cost component does prompt caching leave completely untouched?",
o:["A. Input tokens before the breakpoint on a cache hit","B. Input tokens before the breakpoint on a cache write","C. Output tokens","D. The distribution of input tokens reported across the `usage` counters"],a:2,
e:"Caching is an **input-prefix** optimisation only. **Output tokens are never cached and never discounted**, and output is the more expensive side of the ledger per token. So a workload dominated by long generations — report writers, code generators, verbose agent narration — gains little from caching no matter how well the prefix is engineered. Attack output cost with different levers: tighter output contracts, structured output instead of prose, a `max_tokens` ceiling matched to the shape you actually need, and a cheaper tier where quality permits.",
w:"A — hits are billed at roughly 0.1× base input, a large change rather than none. B — writes are billed at 1.25× base input, and 2× with a 1-hour TTL. D — enabling caching visibly redistributes reported input across `input_tokens`, `cache_read_input_tokens`, and `cache_creation_input_tokens`."},

{id:1494,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An SRE is asked to alarm on prompt-cache hit rate. They search the response for a hit-rate or `cache_hit` field and find none.\n\nWhat is the correct approach?",
o:["A. Enable a caching beta header that adds a `cache_hit_rate` field to `usage`","B. Infer hits from response latency, since a hit is always faster","C. Poll a cache statistics endpoint for the workspace","D. Derive it yourself from `cache_read_input_tokens` and `cache_creation_input_tokens` across requests"],a:3,
e:"There is **no server-provided hit-rate field and no cache statistics endpoint**. The API gives you two per-request counters — **`cache_read_input_tokens`** and **`cache_creation_input_tokens`** — and aggregation is your job. A useful token-weighted definition is `reads / (reads + writes)` over a window; a per-request definition counts calls with a non-zero read. Alarm on that ratio *and* on calls where both counters are zero, the silent signature of a sub-minimum or ineligible prefix. Principle: observability of caching is a client-side responsibility.",
w:"A — no beta header exposes a hit-rate field; the two token counters are the entire interface. B — latency is confounded by prompt length, load, and output length, so it cannot reliably distinguish a miss from a slow hit. C — no such endpoint exists."},

{id:1495,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"How do the two cache write multipliers relate, and when does the longer TTL pay for itself?",
o:["A. Both TTLs write at 1.25×; the 1-hour option costs more only on reads","B. The 5-minute TTL writes at 1.25× base input and the 1-hour TTL at 2×, so the longer TTL pays when reuse gaps exceed five minutes but each entry is still read at least twice","C. The 1-hour TTL writes at 1.25× and the 5-minute at 2×, since shorter entries churn more","D. The 1-hour TTL costs the same to write but bills a storage fee per minute retained"],a:1,
e:"Writes cost **1.25×** base input at the default 5-minute TTL and **2×** with `ttl: \"1h\"`; reads stay at roughly **0.1×** either way, and latency behaviour is identical. So the longer TTL buys survival across gaps at a 60% higher write premium. It pays when your cadence is slower than five minutes but each entry still gets **two or more reads** — at one read per write the 1-hour entry costs 2.1 against 2.0 uncached. Both TTLs are refreshed free on every read, so a prefix under steady traffic rarely needs the 1-hour option at all.",
w:"A — the write multipliers differ (1.25× versus 2×); the read multiplier is what stays the same. C — the multipliers are the other way round: longer retention costs more to write, not less. D — there is no per-minute storage fee; you pay a higher one-off write multiplier."},

{id:1496,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A long-running research assistant uses top-level `cache_control`. Every request shows a substantial `cache_read_input_tokens` **and** a non-trivial `cache_creation_input_tokens`.\n\nHow should this be read?",
o:["A. As an unstable prefix; something in the static content changes on every call","B. As a misconfiguration; reads and writes are mutually exclusive on a correct request","C. As the expected pattern for an advancing breakpoint — everything through the previous turn is read, and the newest turn is written for the next request to read","D. As double billing; the same tokens are charged as both a read and a write"],a:2,
e:"With automatic caching the breakpoint **moves forward** as the conversation grows, so a healthy request looks exactly like this: the settled prefix through the previous turn is a **read** at roughly 0.1×, and the newest assistant-plus-user content is a **write** at 1.25× that the *next* request will read. Reads and writes coexist by design. What signals a genuine problem is the opposite shape — writes on nearly every call with reads near zero, meaning the prefix is unstable or traffic is too sparse to reuse an entry before it expires.",
w:"A — instability shows up as writes *without* matching reads; a large read figure proves the earlier prefix is stable and matching. B — a single request can legitimately read an existing prefix and extend it with a new write. D — the counters are disjoint: read tokens and write tokens are different tokens, not the same ones billed twice."},

{id:1497,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A high-throughput service is throttled by its input-tokens-per-minute rate limit. Each call sends a 40,000-token shared prefix. The team assumes caching is purely a cost lever and plans to buy a limit increase instead.\n\nWhat have they missed?",
o:["A. Cached prefixes are compressed, so fewer tokens travel over the wire","B. Prompt caching raises the input-token rate limit automatically for cached endpoints","C. Cache hits are not deducted against the rate limit, so a high hit rate improves rate-limit headroom as well as cost","D. Rate limits count only output tokens, so the input prefix was never the constraint"],a:2,
e:"**Cache hits are not deducted against your rate limit.** That makes caching a throughput lever, not merely a cost lever: on a 40,000-token shared prefix with a strong hit rate, the tokens counting toward input-tokens-per-minute collapse to the uncached tail and the same quota carries far more requests. Order of operations for a throttled service: stabilise and cache the shared prefix, measure the new consumption against the `anthropic-ratelimit-*` headers, then discuss a limit increase. Note that cache *writes* still consume quota, so a cold fan-out gets no relief.",
w:"A — nothing is compressed; you still transmit the full prefix in every request body. B — no limit is raised; the mechanism is that hit tokens are not deducted from the existing limit. D — rate limits have separate input-token and output-token dimensions, so the input prefix was very much a constraint."},

{id:1498,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A conversational product fires roughly one request every 40 seconds against the same cached prefix all day. A developer proposes moving to `ttl: \"1h\"` so the entry does not have to be rebuilt.\n\nWhat is the correct assessment?",
o:["A. The 1-hour TTL is required; the 5-minute window resets on a fixed schedule regardless of use","B. Both options cost the same, so the longer TTL is the safer default","C. The 1-hour TTL will reduce latency further, because longer-lived entries are served from faster storage","D. Unnecessary — a 5-minute entry is refreshed free on every read, so 40-second traffic keeps it alive indefinitely at the cheaper 1.25× write"],a:3,
e:"The 5-minute TTL is **refreshed on every read, at no additional cost**. A prefix touched every 40 seconds therefore never expires: you pay the **1.25×** write once and read at roughly 0.1× thereafter. Moving to `ttl: \"1h\"` would raise the write multiplier to **2×** to solve a problem this workload does not have. Reserve the 1-hour option for cadences slower than five minutes — irregular batch tails, human-in-the-loop review queues, long agent pauses. One subtlety: the lifetime is measured from the **start** of the request that reads or writes, so a slow streamed response eats into the window.",
w:"A — the window is a sliding idle timeout refreshed by use, not a fixed-schedule expiry. B — the options are not cost-equivalent: the longer TTL writes at 2× rather than 1.25×. C — the two TTLs behave identically with respect to latency; retention length is the only difference."},

{id:1499,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A document-analysis endpoint streams responses that typically take about four and a half minutes. Follow-up requests are issued roughly 45 seconds after each response finishes and reuse the same 5-minute cached prefix, yet they frequently miss.\n\nWhat is the mechanism?",
o:["A. The cache lifetime is measured from the **start** of the request that writes or reads the entry, so a 4.5-minute response leaves under a minute of window once it completes","B. Streaming responses do not refresh a cache entry; only non-streaming ones do","C. The entry is evicted when a response exceeds the model's typical generation time","D. Reading an entry restarts a fresh 5-minute window only once the client has fully consumed the response"],a:0,
e:"The TTL clock starts at the **beginning of the request that writes or reads the entry, not at the end of its response**. Time spent generating counts against the window, so a 4.5-minute stream leaves roughly 30 seconds of usable lifetime, and a follow-up 45 seconds later arrives too late. Two fixes: move to **`ttl: \"1h\"`**, whose 2× write premium is trivially repaid here, or overlap traffic so the next request starts inside the window. General lesson: for long generations, budget the TTL against request *start* times, not response completion times.",
w:"B — streaming refreshes an entry exactly like a non-streamed read; delivery mode is irrelevant. C — there is no eviction rule keyed to generation duration; the fixed lifetime is simply elapsing. D — the window is not restarted on client consumption; it is measured from request start."},

{id:1500,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A compliance review tool is invoked roughly every 12 minutes during working hours, always with the same 60,000-token regulation corpus as its prefix. Each invocation makes a single call.\n\nWhat is the right caching configuration?",
o:["A. Default 5-minute TTL, accepting a rewrite on each call","B. A 1-hour TTL, since a 12-minute gap always exceeds the 5-minute window while each entry is still read several times across the day","C. No caching, because a 12-minute cadence is too sparse for any TTL","D. A 5-minute TTL plus a keep-alive call every four minutes to refresh the entry"],a:1,
e:"A 12-minute gap guarantees a 5-minute entry has expired, so the default TTL degenerates into **paying 1.25× on every call and never collecting a read** — worse than no caching. With `ttl: \"1h\"` the entry survives the gap: one 2× write is followed by roughly four reads per hour at 0.1×, so cached cost per hour ≈ `2 + 4 × 0.1 = 2.4` prefix-units against `5` uncached. The decision rule is arithmetic on **inter-arrival time versus TTL**, and it needs at least two reads per write to beat uncached at the 2× write multiplier.",
w:"A — with every entry expiring unread, the default TTL adds a 25% write premium for no benefit. C — this cadence is exactly what the 1-hour option exists for; abandoning caching leaves a large saving unclaimed. D — synthetic keep-alives cost a read every four minutes forever, roughly 15 an hour to protect four real calls, and add a scheduler to operate."},

{id:1501,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"What does reading a live cache entry do to its remaining lifetime, and at what cost?",
o:["A. Nothing; the lifetime runs from the original write and cannot be extended","B. It extends the lifetime, but only if the read is billed at the write rate","C. It refreshes the lifetime at no additional cost","D. It halves the remaining lifetime as an anti-staleness measure"],a:2,
e:"Every read **refreshes** the entry's lifetime, and the refresh is **free** — you pay only the discounted read tokens at roughly 0.1× base input. The TTL is therefore an *idle* timeout, not a hard expiry measured from the write. The consequence is strategically important: any prefix under steady traffic stays warm indefinitely on the cheap default TTL, so the 1-hour option is for cadences slower than the window rather than for long-lived workloads generally. Detail worth remembering: the refreshed window is measured from the **start** of the refreshing request.",
w:"A — the entry is not pinned to its write time; use extends it. B — reads are billed at the read multiplier, not the write multiplier, and they still refresh. D — nothing shortens the lifetime on access; access lengthens it."},

{id:1502,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"To keep a cached prefix alive around the clock, a team schedules a synthetic \"ping\" request against the prefix every four minutes. Six months later a cost review flags the scheduler.\n\nWhat is the strongest objection?",
o:["A. Synthetic traffic is rejected, because the placeholder message makes the prefix non-identical","B. Keep-alive requests are billed as writes, so the entry is rewritten every four minutes","C. The pings inflate the reported hit rate but do not actually refresh the entry","D. It pays roughly 15 discounted reads an hour, plus output and scheduler overhead, to avoid write premiums that `ttl: \"1h\"` or the real traffic itself would have handled more cheaply"],a:3,
e:"Keep-alive pinging is a common wrong instinct. Each ping is a real billable request — a discounted read of a large prefix plus some output — repeated ~15 times an hour, ~360 times a day, forever, and it needs a scheduler someone must own and monitor. Compare the alternatives: if real traffic already arrives inside the window, **refresh-on-read** keeps the entry warm for free; if it does not, **`ttl: \"1h\"`** buys the same survival for a one-off 2× write. Where a cold *first* request genuinely matters, the supported answer is a **`max_tokens: 0` pre-warm** at start-up, not a perpetual timer.",
w:"A — a ping whose placeholder sits after the breakpoint shares the prefix fine; the objection is cost, not correctness. B — a ping against a live entry is billed as a read, not a write, which is why the waste is subtle rather than obvious. C — pings do genuinely refresh the entry; the problem is that they are a needlessly expensive way to do it."},

{id:1503,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An overnight Message Batches job submits 30,000 requests that all share a 45,000-token reference corpus. On a trial run with the default cache TTL, reads were sporadic and much of the corpus was rewritten repeatedly.\n\nWhat is the recommended change?",
o:["A. Use `ttl: \"1h\"` on the shared prefix, because batch processing can take far longer than five minutes","B. Add three more breakpoints so each shard of the corpus caches independently","C. Set `max_tokens: 0` on the first batch request to pre-warm the corpus for the rest","D. Split the job into 5-minute sub-batches submitted back to back"],a:0,
e:"Prompt caching works with Message Batches, but hits are **best-effort**: requests are processed concurrently and in any order, and a batch may run far longer than five minutes. The documented guidance is the **1-hour cache duration** for batches with shared context, so entries survive the run. Also keep the `cache_control` blocks **identical in every request** in the batch and share as much prefix as possible. The two discounts stack conceptually — batch pricing on the job, cache reads on the shared prefix — which usually makes this the largest single saving available on bulk work.",
w:"B — shard-level breakpoints do not address entries expiring mid-run, and only four slots exist. C — `max_tokens: 0` is rejected inside a Message Batches request; every batched request needs `max_tokens` of at least 1. D — batch completion timing is not controlled by slicing, and 30,000 requests would still outlive a five-minute window."},

{id:1504,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"A request mixes a 1-hour-TTL breakpoint and a 5-minute-TTL breakpoint. What ordering constraint applies?",
o:["A. The 5-minute entry must appear first, since shorter-lived content is more volatile","B. Cache entries with the longer TTL must appear before those with the shorter TTL","C. Mixed TTLs are rejected; all breakpoints in a request must share one TTL","D. Order is unconstrained, but mixed TTLs consume two breakpoint slots each"],a:1,
e:"Mixing is allowed, with one rule: **longer-TTL breakpoints must appear before shorter-TTL ones** — a 1-hour block precedes any 5-minute block. This mirrors how a prefix works: the most durable content sits nearest the front (a corpus or tool catalogue you want held for an hour) and the faster-churning tail behind it (a conversation you only need for minutes). Billing follows the positions: read tokens for the matched prefix, 1-hour write tokens up to the last 1-hour breakpoint, and 5-minute write tokens for the remainder up to the final breakpoint.",
w:"A — the constraint runs the other way; a 5-minute block may not precede a 1-hour block. C — mixed TTLs in one request are explicitly supported, and are a key reason to use explicit breakpoints over the automatic form. D — each breakpoint consumes one slot regardless of TTL, and the ordering constraint is real."},

{id:1505,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"What information does the nested `cache_creation` object inside `usage` provide?",
o:["A. The number of distinct cache entries created, and their identifiers","B. The remaining lifetime in seconds of each entry written","C. A breakdown of written tokens by TTL, as `ephemeral_5m_input_tokens` and `ephemeral_1h_input_tokens`","D. The prefix hash used for lookup, so clients can verify stability"],a:2,
e:"`cache_creation` decomposes the single `cache_creation_input_tokens` figure **by TTL**, reporting `ephemeral_5m_input_tokens` and `ephemeral_1h_input_tokens`. That matters because the two write at different multipliers — **1.25×** and **2×** — so an accurate cost model for a mixed-TTL request needs the split, not the total. It doubles as a correctness check: if you intended an hour-long corpus but the whole write lands in `ephemeral_5m_input_tokens`, your `ttl` never reached the block you thought it did.",
w:"A — no entry count or identifier is exposed; entries are not addressable objects. B — remaining lifetime is not reported anywhere in the response. D — no prefix hash is returned; prefix stability must be verified by controlling your own serialisation."},

{id:1506,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team wants a Message Batches job to start warm, so they make the first request in the batch a `max_tokens: 0` pre-warm carrying the shared prefix. Batch creation fails validation.\n\nWhat is the reasoning behind the restriction?",
o:["A. Pre-warming requires an `anthropic-beta` header, which batch requests cannot carry","B. `cache_control` is not permitted inside batch requests, so there is nothing to warm","C. A batch is submitted as one unit, so its requests cannot be ordered relative to one another","D. Every batched request needs `max_tokens` of at least 1: pre-warming targets time-to-first-token, which does not apply to asynchronous processing, and an entry written mid-batch would likely expire before anything reused it"],a:3,
e:"**`max_tokens: 0` is rejected inside a Message Batches request.** The stated rationale is twofold: pre-warming exists to cut **time-to-first-token**, a metric with no meaning for asynchronous work, and an ephemeral entry written during a batch would probably expire before a follow-up request reached it. The correct batch strategy is different in kind — put identical `cache_control` blocks on the shared prefix in **every** request and use the **1-hour TTL** so entries survive a long run. Hits within a batch stay best-effort because requests execute concurrently and in arbitrary order.",
w:"A — pre-warming needs no beta header; it is an ordinary Messages request with `max_tokens: 0`. B — `cache_control` is fully supported inside batch requests and is the recommended pattern. C — request ordering within a batch is indeed not guaranteed, which weakens the plan, but the validation failure is specifically the `max_tokens: 0` rule."},

{id:1507,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An agent caches its tool catalogue at breakpoint one, a long system prompt at breakpoint two, and settled history at breakpoint three. On a subset of requests it switches `tool_choice` from `{\"type\":\"auto\"}` to `{\"type\":\"tool\",\"name\":\"lookup\"}`.\n\nWhat happens to the cache on those requests?",
o:["A. The entire prefix is invalidated, including tools and system","B. Tools and system stay cached; the message blocks are invalidated","C. Nothing is invalidated; `tool_choice` is not part of the cacheable prefix","D. Only the tool definitions are invalidated, since `tool_choice` refers to them"],a:1,
e:"Changing `tool_choice` invalidates **message blocks only** — the `tools` and `system` caches survive. Mechanically, forcing a tool alters how the conversation is rendered, not the tool definitions or the system instructions. The practical consequence is that a multi-breakpoint layout is *protective*: the two most expensive regions keep hitting even when `tool_choice` varies per request, and you re-pay only for history. Contrast with editing a **tool definition**, which sits at the front of the prefix and therefore invalidates everything downstream — tools, system, and messages alike.",
w:"A — whole-prefix invalidation is what changing tool *definitions* causes; `tool_choice` is narrower. C — `tool_choice` is not a content block, but it does invalidate the message cache, so it is not inert. D — the tool definitions are precisely the part that stays cached; the messages are what break."},

{id:1508,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An agent request carries 14 tool definitions with a breakpoint on the last one, plus a 20,000-token cached system prompt. A feature flag removes one rarely used tool for some accounts. On those requests, `cache_read_input_tokens` drops to zero across the whole prefix.\n\nWhy did the system prompt stop hitting?",
o:["A. `tools` precedes `system` in the prefix, so any change to tool definitions invalidates everything after it","B. Removing a tool drops the request below the minimum cacheable length","C. Tool and system caches are stored jointly and always invalidate together","D. The breakpoint moved to a different tool, which resets all breakpoints in the request"],a:0,
e:"The cacheable prefix is ordered **tools → system → messages**, and an entry is keyed on the exact bytes from the start of the request through the breakpoint. `tools` sits at the very front, so **any** change there — adding, removing, or renaming a tool, or editing a description or schema — changes every prefix downstream and invalidates system and messages too. Design implication: tool catalogues must be **deterministic per cache cohort**. Either serve the same catalogue to everyone and gate capability in your own authorisation code, or accept a separate cache per flag combination and keep those combinations few.",
w:"B — removing one definition from a 20,000-token-plus prefix does not approach any minimum threshold. C — they are not stored jointly; a `tool_choice` change invalidates messages while leaving tools and system cached, which proves the regions are independent. D — breakpoints are declared by you, not relocated by the API, and one changing does not reset the others."},

{id:1509,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A multi-turn assistant caches tools and a long system prompt. On turn six a user attaches a screenshot. That request shows `cache_read_input_tokens` covering tools and system, but the previously cached conversation history is rewritten.\n\nIs this expected?",
o:["A. No — images cannot appear in a request that uses caching","B. No — adding an image invalidates the whole prefix, so tools and system should also have been rewritten","C. Yes — adding or removing images anywhere in the prompt invalidates message blocks, while the tools and system caches survive","D. Yes — but only because the image pushed the prefix past the model's maximum cacheable size"],a:2,
e:"Adding or removing an **image** anywhere in the prompt invalidates **message blocks**, leaving the `tools` and `system` caches intact. Images are themselves cacheable content blocks in user turns, so once the new history settles the next turn caches it again. This belongs to a family of message-scoped invalidators worth memorising together: `tool_choice` changes, image changes, and thinking or `output_config.effort` configuration changes. The whole-prefix invalidators are the ones at the front — tool definition changes, and toggles such as web search or citations that rewrite the system prompt.",
w:"A — images are explicitly cacheable content and compose fine with caching. B — the observed behaviour is correct: image changes are message-scoped, not prefix-wide. D — no maximum cacheable size caused this; the invalidation is a documented consequence of the image change itself."},

{id:1510,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A Python service builds its `tools` array by serialising dictionaries assembled from a plugin registry. Cache hit rate hovers around 30% with no obvious pattern, and the same logical catalogue is sent every time.\n\nWhat is the most likely cause?",
o:["A. Tool definitions are excluded from the cacheable prefix, so hit rate reflects only system caching","B. The registry returns a different number of tools per request","C. Cache entries are evicted at random under load, which caps hit rate","D. The serialised JSON is not byte-stable — key ordering, whitespace, or number formatting varies between requests"],a:3,
e:"A cache lookup is an **exact match on the serialised prefix**. \"Logically identical\" is not enough: differing key order inside an `input_schema`, a changed indent or separator setting, `1.0` versus `1`, or non-deterministic iteration over a set or registry all produce different bytes and therefore a different prefix. Because `tools` sits at the front, the damage propagates to system and messages as well. The fix is a **canonical serialisation** — sorted keys, fixed separators, fixed tool order — computed once and reused, ideally with a hash of the rendered prefix logged so drift becomes detectable rather than mysterious.",
w:"A — tool definitions are cacheable and are the first region of the prefix. B — a varying tool count is one way to break the bytes, but the stem states the same logical catalogue is sent, which points at serialisation rather than membership. C — hit rate is not capped by random eviction; the misses here are deterministic consequences of differing bytes."},

{id:1511,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A cached 18,000-token system prompt ends with the line `Assisting: priya.n (session 4f1a-9c22)`. Traffic is heavy and evenly spread, yet `cache_creation_input_tokens` is large on almost every call and reads are rare.\n\nWhat is the diagnosis?",
o:["A. Interpolating per-user and per-session values into the cached prefix gives every session its own entry, so nearly every call is a first call","B. Session identifiers are hashed by the API, which makes the prefix non-deterministic","C. The prefix exceeds the length at which entries can be shared, so each call writes its own","D. Personal data is excluded from caching for privacy reasons, so that block cannot be cached"],a:0,
e:"Any per-request value inside the prefix **fragments the cache to that value's cardinality**. With a user handle and a session id in the system prompt, each session gets a private entry that is written once and barely read, so you pay the **1.25×** write premium at population scale and collect almost no **0.1×** reads. The fix is structural: keep the prefix strictly **tenant- and session-invariant**, put the breakpoint at its end, and move identity, timestamps, and session context into the volatile tail *after* the breakpoint.",
w:"B — no hashing is applied to your content; the prefix is unshareable because the bytes genuinely differ per session. C — there is a minimum cacheable length, not a maximum beyond which sharing stops. D — caching applies no content-based privacy filtering; nothing is excluded because it looks personal."},

{id:1512,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A support agent caches ten settled turns of history. Compliance then requires redacting a card number that appeared in turn three, so the application rewrites that message and resends the conversation.\n\nWhat is the cache consequence?",
o:["A. Nothing changes, because only the tail after the last breakpoint is compared","B. The prefix diverges from turn three onward, so everything from that point — including the seven later turns — is rewritten, while tools and the system prompt ahead of it still hit","C. The whole prefix including tools and system is invalidated, since message content changed","D. The edited turn is patched in place and the surrounding entry is reused"],a:1,
e:"An entry matches on the **contiguous bytes from the start of the request through the breakpoint**. Editing turn three makes every prefix position after it different, so turns three to ten are re-written at **1.25×** while the untouched regions ahead of the edit — `tools` and `system` — still read at roughly 0.1×. There is no patching or splicing; caches are prefix-keyed, not content-addressed per block. Design consequence: perform redaction and normalisation **before** a turn enters the history you cache, so settled history is genuinely immutable.",
w:"A — comparison covers the whole prefix, not just the tail; that is why an early edit is expensive. C — regions ahead of the edit are unaffected, so tools and system survive. D — there is no in-place patching; a divergent prefix is simply a new prefix."},

{id:1513,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"R",f:"H",
q:"Which change invalidates the `tools`, `system`, **and** message portions of the cache?",
o:["A. Changing `tool_choice` from `auto` to a named tool","B. Attaching an image to the newest user turn","C. Enabling the web search server tool, which modifies the system prompt","D. Raising `max_tokens` from 1,024 to 4,096"],a:2,
e:"Invalidation scope follows **position in the prefix**. Changes that rewrite the front break everything behind them: **tool definition changes**, and toggles that rewrite the system prompt such as **web search** and **citations**, plus the speed setting. Narrower invalidators touch messages only — `tool_choice`, image changes, and thinking or `output_config.effort` changes. Some parameters are not part of the prefix at all and invalidate nothing. Reason from where a change lands in `tools → system → messages` and you can predict blast radius without memorising a table.",
w:"A — `tool_choice` changes affect message blocks only; tools and system stay cached. B — image changes are message-scoped as well. D — `max_tokens` is an output cap, not part of the cacheable prefix, so it invalidates nothing."},

{id:1514,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team must add a short per-request \"today's date\" banner to a workload whose 30,000-token `system` prefix currently caches at a 95% hit rate. They fear this will destroy caching.\n\nWhat placement preserves the hit rate?",
o:["A. Put the banner in the first `system` block and move the breakpoint to the end of `system`","B. Round the date to the nearest hour so the prefix changes less often","C. Send the banner in a separate request first, so it warms independently","D. Put the banner **after** the last breakpoint — a later `system` block or the user turn — leaving the cached prefix byte-identical"],a:3,
e:"Only content **up to and including the breakpoint** participates in the match. Anything placed **after** it is ordinary uncached input and may change freely on every request. So volatile material — dates, request ids, retrieved passages, the current question — belongs in the tail, and the breakpoint marks the boundary between \"stable enough to cache\" and \"changes per call\". The 30,000-token prefix keeps hitting at roughly 0.1×, and you pay full price only for a banner of a few dozen tokens. Design habit: draw the volatility boundary first, then place the breakpoint on it.",
w:"A — putting volatile content at the *front* of the prefix is the worst option: it changes bytes upstream of everything and invalidates the whole cached region daily. B — hourly rounding still rewrites the prefix on a schedule and buys nothing that correct placement gives for free. C — a separate warming request writes a different prefix and cannot help real requests match."},

{id:1515,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An orchestrator reuses one cached prefix — tools, a long system prompt, and settled history — but varies `output_config.effort` per task, using a low setting for triage and a high setting for analysis.\n\nWhat should the team expect from the cache?",
o:["A. Changing the effort value always invalidates message blocks, with a model-specific effect on the tools and system caches; setting effort explicitly to the model's default is equivalent to omitting it","B. Effort is a sampling control outside the prefix, so nothing is invalidated","C. Each distinct effort value maintains a fully independent copy of the entire prefix, so hit rate is unaffected","D. Mixing effort values in one workload returns `400` when `cache_control` is present"],a:0,
e:"**Changing `output_config.effort` always invalidates message blocks**, and its effect on the `tools` and `system` caches is **model-specific** — the same qualification that applies to thinking-configuration changes. One useful detail: setting effort explicitly to the model's **default** is equivalent to omitting it and does **not** invalidate. Practical layout: group work by effort level so each cohort builds and reuses its own message cache, keep the expensive static regions behind their own earlier breakpoints, and verify the counters on your target model rather than assuming.",
w:"B — effort is not a sampling parameter and is not inert with respect to caching; message blocks always invalidate. C — nothing guarantees an independent full prefix copy per effort value, and the message cache demonstrably breaks. D — mixing effort values is legal; the cost is cache churn, not an error."},

{id:1516,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"In review, an engineer objects to marking the **newest** user turn with `cache_control`, arguing that this text is unique to this request so the write is pure waste.\n\nHow should this be answered?",
o:["A. The objection is correct; breakpoints should never sit on content that has not yet repeated","B. The objection is wrong — this request's write becomes the next request's read, which is how a growing conversation chains cache hits turn after turn","C. The objection is correct, because a breakpoint on a user turn is ignored by the API","D. The objection is wrong, because writes on message blocks are billed at the read rate"],a:1,
e:"In a growing conversation the newest turn is unique **now** and part of the settled prefix **next turn**. That is exactly the pattern automatic caching implements: each request reads everything through the previous turn and writes the newest content, so turn *n*'s **write at 1.25×** is redeemed as turn *n+1*'s **read at roughly 0.1×**. Since a single read already repays a 5-minute write, the write is profitable as long as the conversation continues. The objection would hold only for a genuinely one-shot request whose prefix will never be sent again.",
w:"A — content that will repeat on the *next* request is precisely what should be marked; waiting for repetition means never writing the entry that makes reuse possible. C — message content blocks in both user and assistant turns are cacheable and honour `cache_control`. D — writes are billed at the write multiplier regardless of which region they fall in."},

{id:1517,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A shared internal gateway fronts Claude for several business units. To raise hit rate it normalises requests, prepends a common prefix, and caches aggressively — including, on long-context requests, the retrieved documents the caller supplied.\n\nWhat is the serious hazard?",
o:["A. Cross-organisation cache sharing, which the platform permits inside a single gateway","B. Gateway-side caching is unsupported and returns `400` on the second request","C. If the gateway lets one unit's request match a prefix containing another unit's retrieved documents, that content is replayed into the second unit's prompt — a data-exposure defect the platform cannot prevent, because the gateway chose the prefix","D. Cache reads bypass audit logging, so the gateway loses request attribution"],a:2,
e:"The platform isolates caches **between organisations** and, on the Claude API, **per workspace** within an organisation — but it cannot police a *tenant* boundary that exists only inside your gateway. If the gateway builds a prefix containing one unit's confidential documents and later matches another unit's request against it, that content is re-inserted into the second prompt. The correct design keeps the cached prefix strictly **tenant-invariant** — shared instructions and tool catalogue only — puts all caller-supplied material **after** the breakpoint, and uses separate workspaces where separation must be enforced by the platform.",
w:"A — caches are never shared across organisations; the exposure risk here lies entirely within one organisation's own gateway logic. B — gateway-side caching is a normal and recommended pattern; nothing errors. D — cache reads have no effect on logging or attribution; every request is still a request."},

{id:1518,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An agent's tool catalogue is assembled per request by querying a feature-flag service, so the set and order of its 20 tool definitions varies slightly by account and rollout cohort. Cache hits on the system prompt behind the tools are poor.\n\nWhat is the highest-leverage change?",
o:["A. Move the breakpoint from the last tool definition to the first, so at least part of `tools` caches","B. Cache the system prompt in a separate request so it is unaffected by the tools","C. Drop caching on `tools` and cache only `system`, since tool definitions are cheap","D. Send one deterministic, sorted catalogue to every account, gate capability in your own authorisation code, and keep the number of distinct catalogue variants small"],a:3,
e:"`tools` is the **first region of the prefix**, so per-account variation there invalidates `system` and `messages` behind it and multiplies your cache population by the number of variants. Two moves fix it: make the catalogue **deterministic** — fixed sort order, stable serialisation — and make it **uniform**, deciding what an account may actually invoke in your own authorisation layer rather than by withholding definitions. Remember tool definitions are billed as input tokens on every request, so a stable catalogue that caches is cheaper than a trimmed one that does not.",
w:"A — a breakpoint on the first tool caches almost nothing and still leaves `system` sitting behind a varying tools region. B — there is no way to cache `system` independently of the tools preceding it in the same request; a separate request writes a different prefix. C — abandoning the tools breakpoint does not help, because the varying definitions still sit ahead of `system` in the prefix and still break it."},

{id:1519,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A RAG assistant sends tools, a 12,000-token system prompt, then eight retrieved passages, then the user's question. The breakpoint sits on the last retrieved passage. Hit rate is near zero.\n\nWhat is the fix?",
o:["A. Move the breakpoint to the end of `system`, so the per-query passages fall outside the cached prefix","B. Sort the retrieved passages deterministically so the prefix becomes stable","C. Cache the passages with `ttl: \"1h\"` so they survive long enough to be reused","D. Reduce retrieval from eight passages to two so the prefix changes less"],a:0,
e:"Retrieved passages are **per-query by construction** — different questions retrieve different chunks — so any breakpoint after them is keyed on content that essentially never repeats. Move the breakpoint to the end of the last **stable** region, here the system prompt, and let retrieval sit in the uncached tail. You then read 12,000 tokens at roughly 0.1× on every call and pay full price only for passages you genuinely had to fetch. General rule: the breakpoint marks the boundary between request-invariant and request-specific content, and retrieval is almost always on the specific side.",
w:"B — sorting makes an identical *set* serialise identically, but different questions still retrieve different sets, so the prefix still differs per query. C — a longer TTL retains an entry nobody will ever match; retention is not the problem. D — fewer passages still vary per query, so the prefix stays unstable while answer quality drops."},

{id:1520,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"E",
q:"A classification service sends a fixed instruction block plus 30 few-shot examples — about 9,000 tokens, identical on every call — followed by the item to classify, around 80 tokens. Volume is 400,000 calls a month.\n\nWhat is the right caching layout?",
o:["A. Cache nothing; the per-call item is what dominates the request","B. One breakpoint at the end of the few-shot block, leaving the item to classify after it","C. A breakpoint after the item to classify, so the whole request is cached","D. A breakpoint on each few-shot example, for finer-grained reuse"],a:1,
e:"This is the canonical caching shape: a large **request-invariant** prefix and a tiny **request-specific** tail. One breakpoint at the end of the few-shot block means 9,000 tokens read at roughly **0.1×** on nearly every call, with only ~80 tokens billed as ordinary input. At 400,000 calls a month that approaches a 90% cut on the dominant cost component. Few-shot sets and reference documents are ideal cache contents precisely because they are long, stable, and version-controlled — the opposite of retrieved or user-supplied text.",
w:"A — the 9,000-token prefix is over 99% of the input on every call; the 80-token item is the rounding error. C — a breakpoint after the item makes the unique item part of the prefix, so no two requests ever match. D — you have only four breakpoints, and splitting a block that always travels together buys no extra reuse while wasting slots."},

{id:1521,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"What effect does serving a prompt prefix from cache have on the content Claude generates?",
o:["A. Responses become more consistent, because the cached prefix is processed identically each time","B. Responses may drift, since cached activations age","C. Output quality degrades slightly, which is the trade-off for the discount","D. None — caching is a cost and latency optimisation, and responses are the same as without it"],a:3,
e:"Caching changes **what you pay and how fast the first token arrives**, not what the model produces; the documented position is that responses are identical with or without it. Two consequences. First, you never need to eval a cached and an uncached variant of the same prompt as though they were different prompts. Second, when output *does* change between requests, caching is the wrong suspect — look at prompt drift, a model or serving change, or ordinary sampling variation, since Claude's output is not fully deterministic even with sampling controls at their defaults.",
w:"A — caching adds no determinism; run-to-run variation is unchanged. B — there is no ageing or drift mechanism; an entry either matches exactly and is reused, or it does not exist. C — there is no quality trade-off, which is exactly caching's appeal over lossy alternatives such as truncating context."},

{id:1522,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Which statement correctly describes the relationship between prompt caching and conversation state?",
o:["A. Caching makes the API stateful, so only new turns need to be sent","B. Caching stores the conversation for the TTL, and the client resends history only after expiry","C. The API remains stateless: you resend the full history every request, and caching only changes how the repeated prefix is billed and processed","D. Caching replaces history with a server-side summary once the prefix exceeds the model minimum"],a:2,
e:"Prompt caching is **not a memory store**. The Messages API stays **stateless**: every request must carry the entire prompt — tools, system, and the full message history — and caching merely lets the server reuse work on the repeated **prefix** while billing those tokens at roughly 0.1×. Nothing is retrieved by an identifier, nothing persists that you can reference, and a miss simply means the same request is processed at ordinary prices. Consequence for design: your application still owns conversation state, trimming, and summarisation.",
w:"A — statelessness is unchanged; sending only new turns produces a conversation with no history, not a cache hit. B — expiry does not toggle a client behaviour; you always resend everything. D — no server-side summarisation happens, and the model minimum governs cache eligibility only."},

{id:1523,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"A developer reasons that because their prefix is cached, they can send only the newest user message and let the cache supply the rest. What will happen?",
o:["A. The model receives only the newest message, because the request body is the entire prompt and the cache is keyed on a prefix you must still send","B. A `409` is returned, indicating a cache-state conflict","C. The cached prefix is automatically prepended and the request behaves normally","D. The request is rejected until the client resends the full prefix once to re-establish the entry"],a:0,
e:"The cache is looked up **by the prefix you send**, not by a session handle. Sending only the newest message means the prompt genuinely is only that message: no tools, no system instructions, no history — and since the prefix is now different, no cache hit either. You get a coherent-looking but context-free answer, which is worse than an error because nothing fails loudly. The rule follows from statelessness: **transmission is always full; billing is what caching reduces.** You still pay for the newest tokens and save on the repeated ones.",
w:"B — there is no cache-state conflict status; nothing about the request is invalid. C — nothing is prepended on your behalf; the API never reconstructs content you omitted. D — the request is accepted and answered; the failure is silent loss of context, not rejection."},

{id:1524,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"Two teams in one organisation run identical 25,000-token prefixes from separate workspaces, each with its own API key. Their platform lead expects the second team to hit the first team's cache entries. They do not.\n\nWhat is the correct model of cache scope on the Claude API?",
o:["A. Caches are keyed per API key, so a second key never shares entries","B. Caches are isolated between organisations and, on the Claude API, also per workspace within an organisation — so two workspaces do not share entries even with identical prefixes","C. Caches are global across customers for identical prefixes, so this must be a prefix mismatch","D. Caches are shared across an organisation only when both requests use the same model and the same TTL"],a:1,
e:"Isolation is by **organisation and, on the Claude API, by workspace** within it — different organisations never share entries even with byte-identical prompts, and separate workspaces do not share either. Note carefully that the boundary is **not the API key**: two keys in the *same* workspace can share entries, which is why key rotation does not cold-start your cache. Platform note: some deployment surfaces use organisation-level isolation only. So consolidate a genuinely shared prefix into one workspace for hit rate, and split workspaces deliberately when you want the isolation.",
w:"A — keys are not the isolation unit; multiple keys within one workspace share the same cache scope. C — caches are never shared across customers; identical prefixes from different organisations stay separate. D — matching model and TTL do not create cross-workspace sharing; the workspace boundary holds regardless."},

{id:1525,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A batch of 8,000 requests all carry the same `cache_control`-marked 60,000-token corpus with a 1-hour TTL. Results show most requests read from cache, but several hundred recorded writes instead.\n\nHow should this be interpreted?",
o:["A. As a defect; identical `cache_control` blocks guarantee that only the first request writes","B. As evidence the corpus fell below the minimum on some requests","C. As expected: batch requests are processed concurrently and in arbitrary order, so hits within a batch are best-effort and early concurrent requests all miss","D. As TTL expiry; the 1-hour window elapsed partway through the run"],a:2,
e:"Caching works with Message Batches, but hits are explicitly **best-effort**: requests are processed **concurrently and in any order**, and an entry only becomes available once the first response has begun. A wave of simultaneous starts therefore produces several writes before any entry exists to read. Several hundred writes across 8,000 requests is a good outcome, not a bug. Control what you can — identical `cache_control` blocks in every request, the **1-hour TTL** so entries outlive a long run, and as much shared prefix as possible — then accept that exact write counts are not deterministic.",
w:"A — identical cache blocks maximise the chance of a hit but cannot serialise concurrent execution, so a single write is never guaranteed. B — a 60,000-token corpus clears any published minimum, and a sub-minimum prefix would show zero on both counters rather than writes. D — expiry is what the 1-hour TTL is chosen to prevent, and it would cluster writes late in the run rather than at the start."},

{id:1526,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"E",
q:"An analyst uploads a 90,000-token contract, then asks 25 follow-up questions about it over the next few minutes, each in a new request that resends the contract.\n\nWhere should the single cache breakpoint go?",
o:["A. On the final question in each request, so the whole request is cached","B. On the first `system` block, before the contract","C. Nowhere; a document this large exceeds what caching handles","D. On the content block containing the contract, so the contract ends the cached prefix and each question stays outside it"],a:3,
e:"Mark the **last block identical across the requests you want to share an entry** — here, the contract. Each follow-up then reads ~90,000 tokens at roughly **0.1×** instead of paying full input price 25 times, and only the question is billed normally. The arithmetic: one write at 1.25 plus 24 reads at 0.1 is about 3.65 prefix-units against 25 uncached. Because the questions arrive within minutes, refresh-on-read keeps the default 5-minute entry alive throughout, so the cheaper write multiplier is the right choice.",
w:"A — the question is unique per request, so a breakpoint after it makes every prefix unique and nothing ever matches. B — a breakpoint before the contract caches a few hundred tokens and leaves the 90,000 expensive ones re-billed in full every time. C — large documents are the ideal caching target; nothing about this size is a problem."},

{id:1527,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"How do you cache the `tools` region of a request?",
o:["A. Put `cache_control` on the last tool definition in the `tools` array","B. Set a top-level `cache_tools: true` flag","C. Tool definitions are cached automatically and cannot be marked","D. Put `cache_control` on the `tool_choice` object"],a:0,
e:"Tool definitions are ordinary cacheable content: place **`cache_control` on the last tool definition** in the `tools` array and the whole tools region becomes the cached prefix. This usually deserves its own breakpoint, because tool definitions are **billed as input tokens on every request** along with a fixed tool-use system prompt overhead, and because a separate breakpoint means the tools cache survives events that only invalidate messages — a `tool_choice` change, for instance. Remember the ordering: `tools` sits ahead of `system`, so a tools breakpoint covers tools only.",
w:"B — there is no `cache_tools` flag; caching is declared with `cache_control` on content blocks or once at the top level. C — nothing is cached automatically unless you opt in, and tool definitions can certainly be marked. D — `tool_choice` is a request parameter, not a cacheable content block, and cannot carry `cache_control`."},

{id:1528,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team runs a live experiment between two system-prompt variants, splitting traffic evenly. Both variants are cached at the end of `system`. Cost per call rises against the single-variant baseline even though hit rate is still reported as healthy.\n\nWhat is the mechanism?",
o:["A. Concurrent experiments disable caching for the affected endpoint","B. Traffic is split across two distinct prefixes, so each variant sustains half the reuse and pays roughly twice as many write premiums for the same call volume","C. Experiment metadata is added to the prefix, invalidating it on every call","D. Reads of a variant prefix are billed at the write rate until the experiment concludes"],a:1,
e:"Every distinct prefix is a separate cache population. Halving the traffic to each variant halves its **reads per write**, so entries are likelier to expire before reuse and the **1.25×** write premium is paid roughly twice as often for the same total calls. Aggregate hit rate can still look fine while per-entry economics worsen. Mitigations: run variant tests over a **shorter window at full traffic** rather than a long even split, keep the differing text in a **small block after** a shared cached prefix, and monitor writes-per-call rather than hit rate alone.",
w:"A — nothing disables caching during an experiment; the two variants simply cache independently. C — no experiment metadata is injected by the platform; the fragmentation comes from the two different prompt texts. D — reads are always billed at the read multiplier; there is no experiment-dependent pricing."},

{id:1529,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Can a `thinking` block carry `cache_control`?",
o:["A. Yes, and marking it is the recommended way to cache reasoning for reuse","B. Yes, but only when extended thinking is explicitly configured","C. No — thinking blocks cannot be marked directly, though they are cached alongside other content in previous assistant turns and count as input tokens when read back","D. No — thinking blocks are stripped before caching and never occupy prefix positions"],a:2,
e:"You **cannot** attach `cache_control` to a `thinking` block. It is still cacheable content in practice: when a previous assistant turn is replayed inside a cached prefix, its thinking blocks are cached along with everything else and are billed as **input tokens** on a read. So a multi-turn agent does benefit — just not through a breakpoint you place yourself. A related fact worth keeping alongside it: changing the thinking configuration **invalidates message blocks**, with a model-specific effect on the tools and system caches.",
w:"A — no marking is possible, so it cannot be a recommended technique. B — configuring thinking does not make the block markable. D — thinking blocks are not stripped from a cached prefix; they are cached with the surrounding content and billed as input on read."},

{id:1530,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A voice assistant needs faster time-to-first-token. Its requests carry a 50,000-token cached prefix and produce roughly 400 tokens of speech text. After caching is enabled, first-token latency improves noticeably but total response time barely moves.\n\nWhy?",
o:["A. Caching adds a lookup step that offsets its own gains","B. A 400-token output is too short for caching to help","C. Latency gains appear only with the 1-hour TTL, which they have not enabled","D. Caching removes the cost of reprocessing the input prefix, which is prefill work; generating the ~400 tokens is unaffected and now dominates total time"],a:3,
e:"Caching short-circuits **prefill** — the work of processing the input prefix — so its latency benefit lands squarely on **time-to-first-token**, and is most visible with long documents. Per-token generation speed is unchanged, so once prefill is nearly free the remaining wall-clock time is dominated by producing the 400 tokens. To move total latency further you must attack output: shorter responses, streaming so the user hears the start immediately, or a faster model tier. Note the two TTLs behave identically on latency; retention length is their only difference.",
w:"A — the lookup is not measurable overhead; observed first-token latency improved, which is the intended effect. B — output length does not gate the prefill saving; the input prefix is what caching addresses. C — the 5-minute and 1-hour TTLs are documented as behaving the same with respect to latency."},

{id:1531,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"A team's cached prefix is stable, but they vary `max_tokens` per request between 1,024 and 4,096. What is the effect on their cache?",
o:["A. None; `max_tokens` is an output cap and is not part of the cacheable prefix","B. Message blocks are invalidated on every change","C. The whole prefix is invalidated, since `max_tokens` is a top-level request field","D. The prefix is retained but reads are billed at the write rate until `max_tokens` stabilises"],a:0,
e:"The cacheable prefix is built from **content** — `tools`, `system`, and `messages` — so a request parameter that never enters the prompt cannot change the bytes being matched. `max_tokens` caps output and invalidates nothing. Contrast the parameters that *do* alter how the prompt is rendered and therefore break parts of the cache: **`tool_choice`** and image changes hit message blocks; thinking and **`output_config.effort`** changes hit message blocks with a model-specific effect further forward; tool definition changes and system-prompt-modifying toggles break the whole prefix.",
w:"B — message blocks are invalidated by `tool_choice`, image, thinking, and effort changes, not by an output cap. C — being a top-level field does not put a parameter into the prefix; only content blocks are matched. D — read pricing is not conditional on any parameter's stability."},

{id:1532,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"E",
q:"After enabling caching on a low-traffic internal tool, a developer reports \"caching is broken — we were billed for the very first request even though nothing was cached yet.\"\n\nWhat is the correct explanation?",
o:["A. The first request should be free; a billing correction is needed","B. The first request wrote the entry, and writes are billed at about 1.25× base input, so a cold call costs slightly more than an uncached one — the saving comes from later reads at roughly 0.1×","C. Writes are free but reads are billed, so the order of charges is reversed","D. The first request was billed as a read against an empty entry, a known artefact"],a:1,
e:"A cache **write is billed**, at roughly **1.25×** base input (2× with a 1-hour TTL). So the first, cold call is *more* expensive than not caching at all — about a 25% premium on the prefix — and that investment is recovered by subsequent **reads at roughly 0.1×**. A single read already puts you ahead: 1.25 + 0.1 = 1.35 against 2.0 uncached. The only losing case is a write that is never read, which is exactly the risk profile of a low-traffic tool with an unstable prefix.",
w:"A — nothing is free; writing the entry is billable work and the charge is correct. C — the polarity is reversed: writes carry a premium and reads carry the discount. D — there is no read against an empty entry; a miss simply results in a write."},

{id:1533,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An agent fetches a 40,000-token regulatory document through a tool and will reference it across many follow-up turns. A developer wants that content cached but is unsure whether a `tool_result` block can be a breakpoint.\n\nWhat is correct?",
o:["A. Only `system` and `tools` blocks may carry `cache_control`; tool output must be copied into `system` to be cached","B. `tool_result` blocks are cached automatically and cannot be marked","C. Tool output is excluded from caching because it is produced at runtime rather than authored","D. `tool_use` and `tool_result` content blocks in `messages` are cacheable and may carry `cache_control`, so marking that `tool_result` caches the prefix through it"],a:3,
e:"Most blocks in a request are cacheable: tool definitions in `tools`, content blocks in `system`, text in `messages` for **both** user and assistant turns, images and documents in user turns, and **`tool_use` and `tool_result` blocks in either role**. Marking the `tool_result` makes the fetched document the end of the cached prefix, so follow-up turns read it at roughly 0.1× rather than resending 40,000 tokens at full price. Caveat to keep in view: each later turn adds blocks, so watch the 20-position lookback window and add a breakpoint further back if turns are block-heavy.",
w:"A — message-region blocks including tool results are cacheable, and copying runtime output into `system` would also churn a region you want stable. B — nothing in the message region is cached without an opt-in, and tool results can be marked. C — caching is indifferent to how content was produced; it matches bytes."},

{id:1534,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"Two services both report a poor cache hit rate. Service X shows a large `cache_creation_input_tokens` on nearly every call with reads near zero, at 3,000 calls a minute. Service Y shows the same counters but makes only four calls an hour.\n\nWhat distinguishes the two diagnoses?",
o:["A. Both are prefix-instability problems; call rate is irrelevant to hit rate","B. Both are traffic-cadence problems; high-volume services still need the 1-hour TTL","C. X is an unstable prefix — at 3,000 calls a minute a stable prefix could not go unread — while Y is a cadence problem, since four calls an hour never fall inside a 5-minute window","D. X exceeded a per-entry read concurrency limit, and Y fell below the minimum cacheable length"],a:2,
e:"The same counter signature has two different causes, and **call rate discriminates them**. At 3,000 calls a minute an entry would be read constantly if the prefix were byte-identical, so persistent writes mean the bytes differ — an interpolated timestamp or user id, non-deterministic serialisation, a per-account tool catalogue. At four calls an hour the prefix may be perfectly stable and simply expire between calls, which `ttl: \"1h\"` fixes. Diagnostic habit: check whether **inter-arrival time is inside the TTL** before hunting for a cache-buster.",
w:"A — call rate is the key evidence; at very high volume a stable prefix cannot go unread, so instability explains X but not Y. B — a longer TTL does nothing for X, whose entries go unread within seconds because each prefix is unique. D — there is no per-entry read concurrency limit, and a sub-minimum prefix shows zero on *both* counters rather than large writes."},

{id:1535,d:"D2",s:"Prompt Caching",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A request needs a 1-hour entry on a large static corpus and a 5-minute entry on the growing conversation tail. The developer sets `ttl: \"1h\"` on the corpus block and adds a top-level `cache_control` (default 5-minute) to auto-cache the newest turn. The request returns `400`.\n\nWhat is going on?",
o:["A. The last block already carries explicit `cache_control` with a different TTL from the automatic one, which is rejected; declare both breakpoints explicitly, with the 1-hour block before the 5-minute one","B. Mixed TTLs are not supported in a single request under any configuration","C. Top-level `cache_control` cannot be combined with any block-level breakpoint","D. The corpus must use the default TTL, because 1-hour entries may not be combined with message caching"],a:0,
e:"Automatic caching places its breakpoint on the last cacheable block. If that block **already carries explicit `cache_control` with a different TTL**, the two declarations conflict and the API returns **`400`** — whereas the same TTL would simply make automatic caching a no-op. Mixing TTLs is supported, but it requires **explicit** breakpoints, and the ordering rule applies: **longer-TTL blocks must appear before shorter-TTL ones**. So mark the corpus with `ttl: \"1h\"` and the newest turn with a default-TTL breakpoint, keeping the total within four.",
w:"B — mixed TTLs are explicitly supported; they simply cannot be arranged through the automatic convenience. C — the automatic and explicit forms do compose, provided a breakpoint slot is free and no TTL conflict arises on the last block. D — a 1-hour corpus entry combines perfectly well with a shorter-lived message entry when both are declared explicitly in the right order."}
);
